<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Godfrey I of Louvain</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1060–<a href="/years/1139">1139</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a> · Also known as Godfrey the Bearded, Godfrey I of Brabant</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Godfrey I of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leuven">Louvain</a>, called the Bearded, was Count of Louvain and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brussels">Brussels</a> and, from 1106, Duke of Lower <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lorraine">Lorraine</a>. Although he later lost the ducal office, his family kept the title and became the dukes of Brabant. His daughter Adeliza married Henry I of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/godfrey-i-of-louvain.jpg" alt="Godfrey I of Louvain" width="320" height="267" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Christophre Butkens,Abraham van Diepenbeeck,Mattheus Borrekens · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Godffroy1.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Godfrey, also called the Courageous or the Great, was born about 1060, the son of Henry II and Adela of Orthen, a daughter of Count Everard of Orthen. In 1095 he succeeded his brother Henry III, who died wounded in a tournament and left only young daughters. He became Landgrave of Brabant and Count of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brussels">Brussels</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leuven">Leuven</a>, and from 1106 he was also Margrave of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antwerp">Antwerp</a>. Early in his rule he came into conflict with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/otbert">Otbert</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">Bishop</a> of Liège, over the county of Brunengeruz, which Emperor Henry IV allotted to the bishop in 1099.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Godfrey was in favour with the emperor and defended his interests in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lorraine">Lorraine</a>. In 1102 he stopped Count Robert II of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/flanders">Flanders</a> from invading the Cambraisis. After Henry IV died in 1106, his son Henry V, who had been in rebellion, imprisoned the loyal Duke Henry of Lower Lorraine and gave the duchy to Godfrey. The deposed duke escaped and captured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aachen">Aachen</a> but failed to retake the duchy. In 1114, during a rift between the emperor and Pope Paschal II, Godfrey led a revolt in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, and in 1118 he and the emperor were reconciled.</p><p>By marrying his daughter Adeliza to Henry I of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>, who was also the father-in-law of the emperor, he greatly increased his prestige. After Henry V died in 1125, Godfrey supported <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/conrad-count-palatine-of-the-rhine">Conrad of Hohenstaufen</a> against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lothair-iii-holy-roman-emperor">Lothair of Supplinburg</a>, who was elected. Lothair withdrew Lower Lorraine and granted it to Waleran of Limburg, whom Godfrey fought at the Battle of Wilderen, where Godfrey was defeated. He kept the margraviate of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antwerp">Antwerp</a> and the ducal title, which in 1183 became that of duke of Brabant. After the assassination of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-the-good">Charles the Good</a> in 1127 he intervened in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/flanders">Flanders</a> on behalf of Theodoric of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alsace">Alsace</a>, who prevailed against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-clito">William Clito</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Godfrey spent his last years in the abbey of Affligem, died of old age on 25 January 1139 and was buried in the left aisle of the abbey church; a death date of 1140 is an error. He married Ida, a daughter of Count Otto II of Chiny, and had several children, among them Godfrey II of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leuven">Louvain</a>, who succeeded him, and Adeliza, who after Henry I's death married William d'Aubigny, 1st Earl of Arundel. His second wife, Clementia, widow of Robert II of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/flanders">Flanders</a>, bore him no children. By an unknown mistress he had a son, Joscelin of Louvain, who married Agnes de Percy.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Godfrey I of Louvain” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 1106 Emperor Henry V gave Count <b>Godfrey I of Louvain</b> the title of duke of Lower Lorraine, an old duchy that had once covered most of the Low Countries but had by then dissolved into a mass of counties and bishoprics.</blockquote><a href="/empire/duchy-of-brabant">Duchy of Brabant · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Godfrey I of Louvain</b> was named duke of Lower Lorraine in 1106, giving the family the ducal title and a claim to authority over the whole region, and they also styled themselves landgraves of Brabant.</blockquote><a href="/state/county-of-louvain">County of Louvain</a></li><li><blockquote>The emperors used the office to reward loyal families: <b>Godfrey the Bearded's</b> house held it, and Godfrey of Bouillon was duke before leading the First Crusade in 1096.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-lower-lorraine">Duchy of Lower Lorraine</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leuven">Leuven</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/belgium">Belgium</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leuven">Leuven</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/belgium">Belgium</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=50.88,4.7&amp;year=1111" aria-label="Leuven on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4657 399 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Godfrey I of Louvain"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5107 669h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5107 669h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=50.88,4.7&amp;year=1111">What was here in 1111? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
